## Six questions this answers

Headline numbers are from the **holdout**: 20 paired seeds (1000–1019), 52 weeks, run once after the freeze, central calibration variant. Selection-seed numbers appear only where labelled.

**1. Which parts drive AOG risk?**
Of 120 part numbers, {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.count}} are flagged urgent (at least 25 hours a year of material shortage exposure: legacy-history AOG hours plus modelled residual AOG under the recommended plan). Together they account for {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.share_of_aog_hours_pct}} of the legacy (P0) history's AOG hours. The riskiest part is {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.rank1.pn}}, at {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.rank1.risk_hours}} risk-hours a year, with most of its grounding hours at {{RESULT:parts.top_risk.rank1.station}}.

**2. Which forecasting method wins, by demand class? Does the reliability model beat the Croston family?**
Frozen champions by Syntetos–Boylan class (chosen on the selection window): smooth: {{RESULT:forecast.champion.smooth.method}}; erratic: {{RESULT:forecast.champion.erratic.method}}; intermittent: {{RESULT:forecast.champion.intermittent.method}}; lumpy: {{RESULT:forecast.champion.lumpy.method}}. On the holdout, the frozen intermittent-rotable champion ranked {{RESULT:forecast.champion.intermittent_R.holdout_rank}}, which is reported as-is; the policies size rotables on the reliability rate instead, so they do not depend on it.
For removal-driven parts (expendables and rotables, holdout) the exposure-based reliability model scores mean MASE {{RESULT:forecast.reliability.mase}} against {{RESULT:forecast.best_croston_family.mase}} for the best Croston-family method ({{RESULT:forecast.best_croston_family.method}}), a paired difference of {{RESULT:forecast.reliability_vs_croston.delta_mase}} (95% CI {{RESULT:forecast.reliability_vs_croston.delta_mase_ci}}). Verdict: {{RESULT:forecast.reliability_vs_croston.verdict}}. Where it does win is the upper quantile that stocking depends on: {{RESULT:forecast.reliability.pinball_summary}}.

**3. What float and reorder points do we recommend, versus the baseline?**
The recommended plan ({{RESULT:frontier.recommended.id}}) holds {{RESULT:policy.I125.rotable_float_units}} serviceable rotable units, against {{RESULT:policy.P2.rotable_float_units}} under P2 and {{RESULT:policy.P0.rotable_float_units}} under legacy rules (serviceable base stock; the {{RESULT:policy.loop_units}} units seeded in the repair loop are common to all three). Consumable and expendable reorder points change on {{RESULT:policy.P2.rop_changed_lines}} PN-station lines versus P0.

**4. Hub or outstation?**
Mostly hub. P1 sizes every station's float on its own demand and puts {{RESULT:network.P1.units_forward_positioned}} rotable units at outstations. P2 pools the float at AUH and positions only {{RESULT:network.P2.units_forward_positioned}} units forward, where a D0 miss is expensive enough to pay for the unit (P0 has {{RESULT:network.P0.units_forward_positioned}}). P2 cuts AOG hours by {{RESULT:network.P2_vs_P1.aog_hours_reduction_pct}} versus P1 (paired {{RESULT:network.P2_vs_P1.delta_aog_hours}} {{RESULT:network.P2_vs_P1.delta_aog_hours_ci}}) while holding {{RESULT:network.P2_vs_P1.delta_capital_aed}}. At the six outstations alone, {{RESULT:network.outstation_aog_summary}}.

**5. What does the Readiness Frontier recommend?**
The pre-stated rule picks {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.id}} (P2 plus greedy rotable additions). At a spares capital of {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.capital_aed}}, material AOG falls to {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.aog_hours}} a year (95% CI {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.aog_hours_ci}}), against {{RESULT:frontier.baseline.aog_hours}} for the legacy baseline at {{RESULT:frontier.baseline.capital_aed}}. Against P2 it is {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.delta_aog_hours}} {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.delta_aog_hours_ci}} for {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.delta_capital_aed}} of capital, saving {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.net_saving_aed}} a year net of holding cost. Two honest caveats: on the selection seeds the same point scored {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.selection_aog_hours}} (outside the holdout CI), and in the low-stress world {{RESULT:frontier.low_stress.summary}}

**6. What should the planner do this week?**
At week {{RESULT:actions.week.as_of}} the Planner Action Centre raises {{RESULT:actions.week.count_total}} actions, {{RESULT:actions.week.count_urgent}} urgent. The first three in the inbox: {{RESULT:actions.top1.summary}}; {{RESULT:actions.top2.summary}}; {{RESULT:actions.top3.summary}}.

